#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179606 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179744 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "c1924df7-205f-512e-b024-72f5a654f062" :question/math-academy-id "e-9560" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = (y - x)\\,\\mathbf{i} - (x + y )\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = 4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos t, \\qquad\n\ty = 4\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y-x=4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-(x+y)=-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-16(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179607 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179745 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "e9d8e6c2-d346-52fb-918a-5d5c95cd986c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9561" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2 + y^2 = 9.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} +3\\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = 3\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}={cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\cos t, \\textrm d u = -\\sin t\\,\\textrm d t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{1}10{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179608 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "337d9d7e-5488-5e6d-af34-e01d22d3d0b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9574" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6eebbc5ae9f548f5b00de9a12d454fa788884bf73ad46a1065eab0d26330a921.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y = \\dfrac14x^2$ from the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ to the point $\\left(4,4\\right).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\ny = \\dfrac{t^2}{4},\n\\qquad\nt\\in \\left[0,4\\right].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(4,4).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf n$ is indeed a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/2b/2b94e24344f600fc02ec5ecac6c1e0ee4c6a5450132753a0a5ac5fceaa9aa1fc.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})=\\frac{t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (\\frac{t}{2})-(t)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-16-0 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179745 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfd04950-8c76-5e5c-bd3b-57f33b66e8e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177916} {:answer/id #uuid "3d33d012-f495-55e4-a694-7649d9356119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177917} {:answer/id #uuid "470219b9-52ad-5f5d-85b1-f6487d2469f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177918} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3d558e-c45c-5b6d-ba67-dbe452d8b64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177919} {:answer/id #uuid "77a8d59a-d4b3-536c-9bf3-7c9b158878e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{26}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177920}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1f6d5bae-0477-56a7-a550-3209e91d41b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177915}] :question/id #uuid "14039085-c7d5-567d-835b-0c3eaccf0c38" :question/math-academy-id "q-127141" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = y^{2}i + xyj$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4x^{2} + 9y^{2} = 36$."}]}
